#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077} :activity/id #uuid "da7c19c9-a092-5227-8c3d-c60c670a8222" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199560 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $A$ and $B$ are sets, the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs $(a,b)$ whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nMany Cartesian products have natural representations in the Cartesian plane. The most common of these is the Cartesian product $\\mathbb R^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^2 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y) \\: : \\: x,y \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis infinite set contains all ordered pairs $(x,y),$ where $x$ and $y$ can be any real numbers. In other words, $\\mathbb R^2$ contains every point in the Cartesian plane! \n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)\n\nSimilarly, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^3 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y, z) \\: : \\: x,y,z \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndescribes every point in three-dimensional Cartesian space. \n\n![](images/e7/e74fb5948144bc61173e4de73865cb746278bd31fd87e4abf927fa01821ba936.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c18e6618-573b-5560-8d95-58ed56f05155" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7652427-e5ff-5d02-9f23-39039f090aff" :step/math-academy-id 153383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198169 :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "2190f1df-5853-5d8c-85e2-33f40f1f0f24" :step/math-academy-id 153384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199579 :tutorial/content "Cartesian coordinates can also be used to visualize other Cartesian products.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following Cartesian product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^- = \\big\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in\\mathbb N,\\, y\\in\\mathbb R^-\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis product contains every point in $\\mathbb R^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative real number.\n\nTo visualize this Cartesian product, consider the following subset of our Cartesian product:\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\big\\{ (1,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. We can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f6165f66b4149b7b2ea52a3cd686836853d85048ca890e850cb771174a150c57.png)\n\nNext, we consider the subset $S_2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ (2,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-.\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. \n\n![](images/ca/caf2299a2e8009ad5b2776ce67786fa7c7e3bdb57aacbd7df87882f59a2350e2.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3, S_4, S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N\\times {R}^{-} & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-$ as the set of vertical rays shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/033c40b86bfd5cebf24d34de644f632213207386c2b67c559af7594468599e1e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1626e562-8e88-5ff3-bae3-4162438c0086" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8329 :tutorial/title "Cartesian Products of Countably Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "cd371945-c79b-5b78-9017-15c39e4d5ed7" :step/math-academy-id 153387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "48b1fb46-7071-5897-9dd7-a47e1d955f6c" :step/math-academy-id 153388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199584 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch a representation of the set $S= [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane.\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [0, 1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in [0,1],\\, y\\in [0,1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_0 \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}0}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{red}0} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{red}{0}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_{\\color{red}0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general $S_{\\color{blue}{i}} \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{blue}{i}} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{i}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor each ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1],$ $S_{\\color{blue}{i}}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},0)$ to the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},1).$\n\n![](images/28/28b9aa0559635ed4090f10dd0b59f2cbb6c297f8736d9509a637b5d78d27561c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I = [0,1]{:}$\n\n$$\nS = \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}.\n$$\n\nAs $\\color{blue}i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}{:}$\n\n![](images/76/76ff48755dbfc3ba1943665d08aa8cfd00948bbc1ddfaab560a0af64f11bae26.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "923fb377-d694-5181-8561-c57612f15054" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8339 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product [0,1] x [0,1]"} :step/id #uuid "ef01039c-9cce-558a-8067-2ffba087e848" :step/math-academy-id 153392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198205 :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ddfd97d-7a1a-59ef-8492-3fbe4088f22f" :step/math-academy-id 153393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198206 :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5777ec1b-f050-570f-86e4-1e7bffa77dfb" :step/math-academy-id 153396}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products"